title The largest fall the arc has taken, and the sigil that reads as a name
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, deep-read one council-rota row, send each finished increment signed.
think Standfast opened the lap. Every REDS row read CLOSED through %130, so the ledger looked clear -- yet running the Caravan choir before touching anything found it RED at tools/caravan_ladder_carry_witness.rish, and a git stash proved the tree was already red at HEAD rather than red from this lap's edits. That became REDS %131.
think The running thread came off the top three logs. The newest recommend named renew_path at thirty-one rungs by twenty-seven lines -- 720 by realized fall, zero widening -- as the measured head, the first head chosen by REDS %130's new meter rather than by a hand-written column.
think Verified the head before moving a line. tools/fixtures/ladder_reach_visibility_scan.sh printed REACH_OK family=renew_path folding=31 widens=0 stub=3 fall=720, and hashing all thirty-one bodies gave one digest, so the family was genuinely identical.
think Lifted the body. pub fn renew_path moved into caravan/ladder_checks.rye taking the rung as a comptime type parameter and reaching max_note_path_len, NoteError, addressable, and reader_dir through the type handed in. Each of the thirty-one rungs now calls it through a three-line stub.
think Ratcheted the meters that pin the measurement. The carry scan's default ceiling came down 75,205 to 74,485; the carry witness's two pinned lines moved to the measured carry and body counts; the reach witness repointed from the folded family to the new head.
obs Prediction met measurement to the digit for the eleventh lap running. Carry fell 75,199 to 74,479 -- a fall of exactly 720 -- while delegates rose 2,922 to 2,953, exactly the thirty-one stubs, which is how a reader tells a fold from a cost moved somewhere cheaper to count. Bodies 18,164 to 18,165 and distinct 7,961 to 7,962, the one published body the fold added. Families hold at 705.
obs This is the largest realized fall the arc has taken, and it stood four laps unchosen. The queue had recorded it as widening thirty-one declarations because fn renew_path stands private in all thirty-one rungs -- while its body reaches only symbols already public everywhere. The meter built last lap found it; this lap collected it. That is what a measurement is worth over a sentence.
obs The reader-box path family closes with it. hand_path (.said), tell_path (.outof), and renew_path (.still) all name an address under the readers' own directory in a five-byte suffix, and hand_path's own doc comment had named the merged body as worth its own lap. All three now stand as one shape in the harness.
obs REDS %131 is the honest find of the lap, and its shape is uncomfortable. The previous lap closed %130 correctly, then wrote (REDS %130) into two say lines of the carry meter AFTER the choir had sung, and shipped signed with a log claiming the choir GREEN at 108. Both halves were true when measured and false when sent. A witness proves the tree it ran on, so any edit after the run reopens the claim.
obs The deeper fault was the guard's own bound rather than the edit. caravan_ladder_prose_count_scan.sh reads every digit run in a meter's say lines, and % is the sigil this tree assigns to a number it names itself -- a constant term, exactly itself, never varying. A ledger row is immutable by the ledger's first law, so it can never drift, which is the only fault that scan exists to catch. The scan now strips %<digits> before reading numbers, and the bound stays tight: a bare count standing beside a citation is still refused.
obs The control for that fix went wrong once and was caught by its own refusal leg. printf reads a bare % as a format conversion and truncated the whole generated say line, so both legs passed while testing an empty sentence -- the welcome for having nothing to refuse, the refusal for having no count left to find. Written %% now, and the failure that exposed it is recorded in the fixture comment.
obs Council rota, row four -- Earth and Mercury, the concrete. The one-clock naming law and the marked value both say the same thing from different sides: a mark carries a stamp and a name, and a value carries what it measures. This lap's whole argument is that shape -- 720 is a marked value read off a scan, while thirty-one was a described one, and the described one cost four laps. Two Rooms named the rest: the widening column moved from the proposed room to the checkable one last lap, and this lap is the first to collect on that move.
obs Every gate green on a quiet tree. Choir 108, era suite ten rungs, TAME bans clean, width clean, living-card ASCII GREEN, radiant-negation GREEN, both REDS ledger guards GREEN with the headline moved 130 to 131 off the measured spine.
